## Migration: `TimedExecutor` — Thread.Abort to Cooperative Cancellation

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone. The entire preemptive-kill model is removed.

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `thread.Abort()` | Timeout enforcement | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Remove entirely |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        // Wrap the Func<T> to accept a CancellationToken if needed later.
        // For now, we enforce the timeout at the orchestration level.
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when the
            // token fires, even if Task.Run's body doesn't check it.
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            // Unwrap single-exception AggregateException from Task.Run
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | `Task.WaitAsync` stops *waiting*, but the delegate keeps running on the thread pool. If `work` is a tight CPU loop, it won't terminate. Pass a `CancellationToken` into the work and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaking it causes timer leaks from `CancelAfter`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at this top-level orchestration point. The `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` guard distinguishes your timeout from an unrelated external cancellation. |
| **`AggregateException` wrapping changed** | `Task.Run` wraps exceptions in `AggregateException`. The original code threw `new AggregateException(error)` directly — the migrated version preserves that behavior. |

### Improved Version (Fully Cooperative)

If you control the work delegate, accept a `CancellationToken` so work **actually stops**:

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}

// Usage:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### If Work Is Truly Uncooperative

If you cannot modify the work to check a token (e.g., third-party library, native call), move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` on timeout. That is the only way to forcibly terminate work in modern .NET — there is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort`.
